  "kind": "build",
  "title": "# Build: model-thinking-audit (FABLE: judging which work is foundation is itself a Fable call) ## W…",
  "asked": "# Build: model-thinking-audit (FABLE: judging which work is foundation is itself a Fable call) ## What Brett said (verbatim, Nov 4 2026, voice) After asking why Fable vs Opus matters and what turning up the thinking level does: \"Go through my list of things that can be done and see what should be redone with Fable and put that next to it, and put the level of thinking that it should be on.\" Book to a free named agent. ## Do 1. **Cover every item**: queued builds (agent-slots queue), pending builds, Plan Studio plans/ideas, and **already-built / merged work from the last ~2 weeks** that was done on Opus but touches foundation (shared asset types, schemas, contracts, engines/kernel, cross-app protocols). Several build reports already flag 'should get a Fable review' (e.g. banner-ref-links, plugin-settings-placement settings-placement@1, xlayout-opus, token-control-opus). Find them all by grepping _claude/builds/reports/. 2. **For each item, record**: recommended model (Fable / Opus), **thinking level** (low / medium / high / max), and a one-line why. For built items, add a verdict: **keep**, **Fable review** (review only, fix if needed) or **redo on Fable**, plus a rough cost/time. Rule of thumb: foundation or contract work = Fable, high or max. Plugins and screens = Opus, medium. Wording, small fixes and landing PRs = Opus, low. Gnarly debugging or architecture inside a plugin = Opus, high. 3. **Put it next to each item where Brett looks**: add `recommendedModel`, `thinking` and `modelWhy` fields to builds/plans in the store, and show them as columns/chips in the Build manager, Builds, Pending builds, Queue and Plan Studio. Also add a **'Fable candidates'** filter. 4. **Don't change any build's model or start any redo.** Recommendations only. Brett decides. Make 'Apply recommendation' a one-tap per row (and a bulk action), owner-only. 5. If the launcher supports a thinking/effort setting, wire `thinking` through to build_start. If not, add the field and note it in the report as a follow-up. 6. Coordinate with `fable-review-nov3` (already queued) via THREADS so the two don't duplicate work. That build can consume this list. 7. Report in _claude/builds/reports/model-thinking-audit.md: a table of every item with model, thinking, verdict and why, plus the top 5 'redo on Fable' picks with reasons. PR, auto-land, redeploy. Commits clean. ## ADDENDUM 1 (Brett, voice, Nov 4) \"I also want to see in the steps list the same thing, and anywhere a build or a step is shown I want a check mark if it's all set and doesn't need to be looked at, and like a yellow triangle if it's been changed and needs to be looked at. And if you right-click that icon, instead of expanding to its information, expand the row explaining why. And all that stuff about why you should change it.\" - **Per step too**: every build step gets its own recommended model, thinking level and why, shown in every steps list. - **Status icon on every build AND step, everywhere one is shown** (Build manager, Builds, Pending, Queue, Plan Studio, Crew/job rows, Home cards, Requests ledger, Codebase changes, Git board): **green check** = all set, nothing to review. **Yellow triangle** = the recommendation differs from what it's on (or it changed) and needs a look. - **Right-click / long-press the icon** opens an inline expansion under the row (NOT the normal details) explaining why: current vs recommended model and thinking, the reasons, what could go wrong if left as is, cost/time to change, and Apply / Dismiss buttons. Dismiss turns it into a check and records that Brett OK'd it as is. - One shared component for the icon + why-panel so every screen behaves the same. Icons come from tokens. ## ADDENDUM 2 (Brett, voice, Nov 4) \"Model. And thinking model. Not just the review, any query or anything that shows a build or a step or anything anywhere. But make it a filter. Make it so you can turn that feature on or off.\" - **Every place a build or step appears** shows two chips: **model*…",
